<div class="html" id="tag">
<article class="env-proposition" id="06WT">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06WT">Proposition <span data-tag="06WT">9.6.7.4</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category containing $2$-simplices $\sigma $ and $\sigma '$, which we depict as </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ &amp;  Y \ar [dr]^{g} &amp;  &amp;  &amp;  Y' \ar [dr]^{g'} &amp;  \\ X \ar [ur]^{f} \ar [rr]^{h} &amp;  &amp;  Z &amp;  X' \ar [ur]^{f'} \ar [rr]^{h'} &amp;  &amp;  Z'. } \]
</div>
<p> If $f$ is left orthogonal to $g'$, then the restriction map </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \theta : \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( \sigma , \sigma ' ) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( h, h' ) \]
</div>
<p> is a homotopy equivalence. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $T: \Delta ^1 \times \Delta ^1 \times \Delta ^2 \rightarrow \Delta ^2$ denote the morphism of simplicial sets given on vertices by the formula 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[ T(i,j,k) = \begin{cases}  i &amp;  \text{ if $k = 0$} \\  j+1 &amp;  \text{ if $k=1$} \\  2 &amp;  \text{ if $k=2$.} \end{cases} \]
</div>
<p>
       Then $(\sigma ' \circ T): \Delta ^1 \times \Delta ^1 \times \Delta ^2 \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ can be identified with a commutative diagram in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$, which we depict as 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \sigma ' \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  s^{1}_{0}(g') \ar [d] \\ s^{1}_{1}(h') \ar [r] &amp;  s_{1}^{1}(g'). } \]
</div>
<p>
       This diagram is a levelwise pullback square, and therefore determines a pullback diagram of morphism spaces 
    <p>
<div class="equation" id="06WU">
<span class="equation-label"><a data-tag="06WU" href="/tag/06WU">9.46</a></span>
<div>\begin{equation}  \begin{gathered}\label{equation:annoying-proof} \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( \sigma , \sigma ') \ar [r] \ar [d]^{ \widetilde{\theta } } &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( \sigma , s^{1}_{0}(g') ) \ar [d]^{\widetilde{\theta }_0} \\ \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( \sigma , s^{1}_{1}(h') ) \ar [r]&amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( \sigma , s_{1}^{1}(g') ) } \end{gathered} \end{equation}</div>
</div>
<p>
       (Proposition <a data-tag="039L" href="/tag/039L">7.4.1.22</a>). Using Corollary <a data-tag="046J" href="/tag/046J">8.3.5.8</a>, we see that $\widetilde{\theta }$ fits into a commutative diagram 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( \sigma , \sigma ') \ar [r] \ar [d]^{ \widetilde{\theta } } \ar [r]^-{\theta } \ar [d] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( h, h') \ar [d]^{\operatorname{id}} \\ \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( \sigma , s^{1}_{1}(h') ) \ar [r] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( h, h'), } \]
</div>
<p>
       where the right vertical map is an identity morphism and the lower horizontal map is a homotopy equivalence by virtue of Corollary <a data-tag="030M" href="/tag/030M">7.3.6.9</a> (since the degenerate $2$-simplex $s^{1}_{1}(h')$ is right Kan extended from the full subcategory $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \{  0 &lt; 2 \}  ) \subset \Delta ^2$). Consequently, the morphism $\theta $ is a homotopy equivalence if and only if $\widetilde{\theta }$ is a homotopy equivalence. Since the diagram (<a data-tag="06WU" href="/tag/06WU">9.46</a>) is a pullback square, it will suffice to show that $\widetilde{\theta }_0$ is a homotopy equivalence (Corollary <a data-tag="048F" href="/tag/048F">7.6.2.39</a>). We may therefore replace $\sigma '$ by $s^{1}_{0}(g')$, and thereby reduce to the case where $\sigma '$ is left-degenerate (Example <a data-tag="04ZP" href="/tag/04ZP">1.1.2.8</a>). Similarly, we may replace $\sigma $ by $s^{1}_{1}(f)$ and thereby reduce to the case where $\sigma $ is right-degenerate. In particular, we may assume that $\sigma $ is left Kan extended from the full subcategory $\Delta ^1 \subset \Delta ^2$. Using Corollary <a data-tag="046J" href="/tag/046J">8.3.5.8</a> again, we obtain a commutative diagram 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( \sigma , \sigma ') \ar [r] \ar [d]^{ \widetilde{\theta } } \ar [r]^-{\theta } \ar [d] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, f' ) \ar [d]^{\operatorname{id}} \\ \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( \sigma , s^{1}_{1}(h') ) \ar [r] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, h'), } \]
</div>
<p>
       where the horizontal maps are homotopy equivalences (Corollary <a data-tag="030M" href="/tag/030M">7.3.6.9</a>). Since $\sigma '$ is left-degenerate, we have $f' = \operatorname{id}_{Y'}$ and $h' = g'$. We are therefore reduced to proving that the natural map $\operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, \operatorname{id}_{Y'} ) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, g' )$ is a homotopy equivalence, which is a reformulation of our assumption that $f$ is left orthogonal to $g'$ (Lemma <a data-tag="06WS" href="/tag/06WS">9.6.7.3</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
